SECTION 2: PODS → REPLICASETS → DEPLOYMENTS → ROLLING UPDATES → ROLLBACKS
Pods
What is a Pod?

A Pod is the smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes.

A Pod can contain:

One Container
OR
Multiple Containers

Most commonly:

1 Pod = 1 Container
Why Pods?

Kubernetes does not manage containers directly.

It manages Pods.

Container
    ↓
Pod
    ↓
Kubernetes
Ecommerce Example

Frontend:

frontend-pod
   |
   +-- Angular Container

Backend:

backend-pod
   |
   +-- Node.js Container

Database:

mysql-pod
   |
   +-- MySQL Container
Pod Lifecycle
Pending
   ↓
ContainerCreating
   ↓
Running
   ↓
Succeeded / Failed
Common Pod States
Pending

Pod created but not scheduled.

Example:

Insufficient CPU
Node Affinity Mismatch
Taint Issue
Running

Application is healthy.

Failed

Application crashed.

Completed

Usually Jobs or CronJobs.

Example:

Database Backup Job
