Rolling Updates
What is Rolling Update?

Update application without downtime.

Old Pods replaced gradually.

Example

Current Version:

Backend v1

Deploy New Version:

Backend v2

Process:

v1 Pod
v1 Pod

↓

v2 Pod
v1 Pod

↓

v2 Pod
v2 Pod

Users experience:

Zero Downtime
Strategy Used in Your Project

Check:

kubectl get deployment ecommerce-backend \
-n ecommerce -o yaml

You observed:

strategy:
  rollingUpdate:
    maxSurge: 25%
    maxUnavailable: 25%
Meaning
maxSurge

Extra Pods allowed during update.

Example:

Current Pods = 4

maxSurge = 25%

Extra = 1 Pod

Temporary:

5 Pods
maxUnavailable

Maximum unavailable Pods.

Example:

4 Pods

25% = 1 Pod

Only 1 pod can be unavailable.

Commands

Watch Rollout

kubectl rollout status deployment/ecommerce-backend

Rollout History

kubectl rollout history deployment/ecommerce-backend
Interview Question
Q5. What is Rolling Update?

Answer:

Rolling Update gradually replaces old Pods with new Pods while keeping the application available.

Rollbacks
What is Rollback?

Revert application to previous version.

Example

Deploy:

Backend v2

Bug found:

Users cannot login

Rollback:

Back to v1
Commands

Deployment Rollback

kubectl rollout undo deployment/ecommerce-backend
Check Revision History
kubectl rollout history deployment/ecommerce-backend
Helm Rollback (Your Project)

Check History:

helm history ecommerce -n ecommerce

Rollback:

helm rollback ecommerce 25 -n ecommerce

You already practiced:

helm history
helm status
helm rollback
Interview Question
Q6. Difference between Kubernetes Rollback and Helm Rollback?

Answer:

Kubernetes rollback reverts Deployment revisions.

Helm rollback reverts the entire Helm release including Deployments, Services, ConfigMaps, and other resources.

